  "brief_md": "# Evidence Brief: A01 — Neutral Third-Party API Breaking-Change Agent\n\n## Idea\nA neutral third-party agent scans customer codebases, tracks breaking changes across API vendors, identifies affected call sites, and opens PRs with fixes. Source: YC RFS (https://www.ycombinator.com/rfs).\n\n## Payer\nEngineering leads at startups/SMBs juggling many third-party API dependencies. Price hypothesis: $14-150/mo per org, anchored to Nalvis's hosted tier (from $14/mo, https://nalvis.dev/) and Since.dev's Studio tier ($149/mo, https://since.dev/).\n\n## Competitors\nThe kill gate undercounted the field's density. Confirmed via direct fetch:\n- **Nalvis** (https://nalvis.dev/): free open-source GitHub Action engine + hosted watcher from $14/mo. Pre-launch/waitlist. Covers Algolia, Clerk, Svix at v1; Stripe/Shopify/Twilio 'upcoming.'\n- **Since.dev** (https://since.dev/): Free / Pro (BYO model key, price not disclosed) / Studio $149/mo for 15 repos. Positions itself as 'self-repairable software' with MCP integration for agents.\n- **mendapi** (https://mendapi.com/): free, open-source (AGPL), local-only CLI scanner + fixer; hosted monitoring is waitlist-only, no price yet. Explicitly targets fintech (Stripe/Plaid/PayPal) with 'audited auto-fix packs.'\n- **ApiPatch** (https://github.com/MoradMoqbel/apipatch, https://pypi.org/project/apipatch/): open-source autonomous agent, published on PyPI, framed as a portfolio/demo project (claims PRs merged into Google Cloud subprojects).\n- **Ripple, Sync, api-guardian**: GitHub repos with no discoverable pricing (NOT FOUND after search).\n\nAll of the well-developed entrants (Nalvis, Since.dev, mendapi) appear to have launched or gained visibility within a similar window in 2026, several citing security/trust ('code never leaves your machine,' 'no auto-merge') as their primary differentiator rather than detection technology.\n\n## Acquisition channels\n1. **GitHub Marketplace** — free to list; GitHub takes a platform cut once revenue passes $500/mo, requires verified-publisher onboarding for paid plans (https://docs.github.com/en/apps/github-marketplace/selling-your-app-on-github-marketplace/receiving-payment-for-app-purchases).\n2. **Dev.to content marketing** — the exact channel mendapi and other builders already use to publish build-story and pain-point posts (https://dev.to/mendapi/your-api-provider-shipped-a-breaking-change-heres-how-to-find-what-it-breaks-in-your-code-3657); near-zero cash cost.\n3. **Product Hunt launch** — no listing fee but ~$3,300-$6,000 in founder opportunity cost for a properly prepared launch (https://uprowshub.com/campaigns/blog/product-hunt-launch-costs).\n\n## Demand evidence\n- \"Twice this year, we got user complaints before we even knew something was broken. The culprit both times? Silent breaking changes from upstream APIs.\" (https://dev.to/kriisht/we-got-burned-by-silent-api-changes-twice-this-year-how-do-you-handle-this-17pl)\n- \"Stripe had quietly changed the structure of their payment_intent.succeeded payload... No announcement. No deprecation warning.\" (https://dev.to/kubabuilds/how-stripe-silently-broke-my-production-app-and-how-i-built-a-fix-p7i)\n- \"Your tests won't catch this because your tests use static fixtures you wrote months ago. Standard monitoring won't catch it.\" (https://dev.to/malikad778/how-a-silent-api-update-broke-our-billing-and-how-to-prevent-it-31ob)\n\n## Case for\nThe pipeline (watch → diff → AST-match → codemod/AI-draft → PR) is a bounded, automatable workflow well-suited to a solo builder using AI agents; several near-solo teams have already shipped working MVPs in this exact space within weeks, proving technical feasibility. Real, recurring, and independently-voiced developer pain exists across major API vendors (Stripe, Shopify, Twilio, OpenAI). Distribution is nearly free via GitHub Marketplace and dev.to — channels competitors are already using successfully to generate visibility.\n\n## Case against\nAt least seven near-identical products (several explicitly derived from the same YC RFS) have appeared within months of each other, several already open-sourcing their core detection engine for free, which puts structural downward pressure on the $14-150/mo price anchor. The real bottleneck isn't detection — it's earning the trust needed for a startup to grant a neutral third party PR-opening access to production code, which every serious competitor addresses with heavy security messaging ('no auto-merge,' 'code never leaves your machine,' local-only scanning) that takes longer than six weeks to establish credibly as a solo operator. Vendors themselves (e.g., Stripe's own upgrade/changelog tooling) are also moving to solve this natively, threatening to commoditize the entire category.\n\n## Open questions\n- Will teams grant write/PR access without extensive security vetting?\n- What distinguishes a new entrant from Nalvis/mendapi/Since.dev, all of which already exist and partly overlap in provider coverage?\n- Does the $14-150/mo price hold once free, self-hostable engines are available?\n- How many providers must be covered before a typical SMB (5-10 API dependencies) sees enough value to pay?\n- Are API vendors planning native tooling that removes the need for a third party?\n\n## Regulatory setup\n- One-time: draft a data-handling/privacy policy and GitHub App permission-scope disclosure before requesting repo access (1-2 hr legal consult), per GitHub Marketplace requirements.\n- Ongoing: SOC 2 Type II or equivalent security posture is commonly expected before SMB/enterprise customers grant broad code-access permissions — an ongoing compliance program, not a one-time task, and likely out of scope for a 6-week build.\n"
